```
This structure separates concerns and prevents the application from becoming a single monolithic source file.

---

## 8. Terminal Layer

The terminal layer is the most important foundation.

ZetaTerminal should use a proper terminal backend capable of PTY-backed shell sessions. For Qt-based development,
QTermWidget is a practical option.

The terminal backend should support:

- Persistent shell process
- PTY interaction
- Environment variables
- Working directory
- ANSI rendering
- Scrollback
- Font configuration
- Color schemes
- Keyboard input
- Clipboard actions
- Terminal resizing

The terminal layer should not depend on the AI layer. It must be possible to use ZetaTerminal normally with no AI
features enabled.

---

## 9. AI Layer

The AI layer communicates with the local model server.

Primary responsibilities:

- Connect to Ollama
- Send chat requests
- Stream or receive responses
- Parse responses
- Handle network errors
- Handle invalid JSON
- Support timeout and cancellation
- Maintain model settings
- Provide connection status

The AI layer should not execute commands directly. It should only produce responses, plans, explanations, or structured
command proposals.

---

## 10. Agent Layer

The agent layer turns AI output into controlled action.

Responsibilities:

- Manage AI task lifecycle
- Request plans from AI
- Validate structured responses
- Classify command risk
- Ask the user for approval
- Execute approved commands
- Capture output
- Feed observations back to AI
- Continue, stop, or revise the task
- Produce final summaries

The agent layer is where safety rules are enforced.

---

## 11. Controlled Command Execution

AI-generated commands should not be executed directly without passing through a controlled command executor.

A command execution request should include:
```
json
{
  "command": "systemctl status docker --no-pager",
  "working_directory": "/home/user",
  "timeout_seconds": 15,
  "requires_confirmation": false
}
```
A command execution result should include:
```
json
{
  "command": "systemctl status docker --no-pager",
  "exit_code": 3,
  "stdout": "...",
  "stderr": "",
  "timed_out": false,
  "duration_ms": 842
}
```
The result can then be sent back to the AI as an observation.

---

## 12. Visible Terminal vs AI Executor

ZetaTerminal should support two execution paths.

### 12.1 Visible Terminal

The visible terminal is for normal user activity and interactive programs.

Use it for:

- Manual commands
- Editors
- SSH sessions
- REPLs
- Full-screen terminal tools
- Interactive prompts

### 12.2 AI Command Executor

The AI command executor is for controlled, non-interactive tasks.

Use it for:

- Diagnostics
- File inspection
- Package checks
- Service status checks
- Build/test commands
- Approved remediation steps

The AI executor should mirror commands and outputs into an execution log so that the user can see everything.

This separation prevents the AI from interfering with the user's active shell while still allowing reliable output
capture.

---

## 13. AI Workflow

A typical AI task should follow this process:
```
text
User request
  ↓
Context collection
  ↓
AI plan generation
  ↓
Plan review
  ↓
Risk classification
  ↓
User approval
  ↓
Command execution
  ↓
Output observation
  ↓
AI plan revision
  ↓
Completion summary
```
Example:
```
text
User: Docker is not starting. Diagnose and fix it.
```
The AI might first propose:
```
bash
systemctl status docker --no-pager
systemctl status containerd --no-pager
journalctl -u docker --no-pager -n 100
```
After observing the output, it may propose:
```
bash
sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl restart docker
```
The restart commands should require explicit confirmation because they modify system service state.

---

## 14. Command Risk Model

AI-proposed commands should be classified independently by the application.

The AI's own risk label should not be trusted as the final authority.

---

### 14.1 Read-Only Commands

These are typically safe diagnostic commands:
```
bash
pwd
ls
cat
head
tail
df -h
free -h
uname -a
whoami
id
ps aux
systemctl status docker --no-pager
journalctl -u docker --no-pager -n 100
git status
```
These may be eligible for automatic execution if the user enables that mode.

---

### 14.2 Medium-Risk Commands

These require confirmation:
```
bash
mkdir
touch
cp
mv
chmod
chown
pip install
npm install
git checkout
git pull
systemctl restart docker
```
---

### 14.3 High-Risk Commands

These require explicit confirmation:
```
bash
sudo apt install package
sudo dnf install package
sudo pacman -S package
rm file
rm -rf directory
docker system prune
systemctl stop service
systemctl disable service
chmod -R
chown -R
curl URL | sh
wget URL | sh
```
---

### 14.4 Blocked or Extremely Dangerous Commands

These should be blocked by default or require a special override:
```
bash
rm -rf /
rm -rf /*
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/sda
mkfs.ext4 /dev/sda
chmod -R 777 /
chown -R user:user /
:(){ :|:& };:
```
---

## 15. AI Autonomy Modes

ZetaTerminal should support configurable autonomy.

### Mode 1: Ask Only

The AI answers questions but does not propose execution.
```
text
User asks a question.
AI explains.
No commands are run.
```
---

### Mode 2: Plan Only

The AI creates a plan, but commands are not run.
```
text
AI produces diagnostic and remediation steps.
User may copy commands manually.
```
---

### Mode 3: Confirm Each Command

The AI proposes commands one at a time. The user approves each command before execution.

Recommended early default.

---

### Mode 4: Auto-Run Read-Only Diagnostics

The AI may automatically run read-only commands but must ask before making changes.

Recommended mature default.

---

### Mode 5: Agent Mode

The AI can run multi-step diagnostic and remediation workflows, while still stopping for high-risk commands.

This should only be enabled after the risk classifier, executor, logging, and approval system are mature.

---

## 16. Safety Policy

The safety policy should be strict by default.

Recommended defaults:
```
text
Ask before sudo: always
Ask before deletion: always
Ask before package install: always
Ask before service changes: always
Ask before permission changes: always
Ask before network download execution: always
Allow automatic read-only diagnostics: optional
Allow destructive commands: off by default
```
The user should be able to cancel any running AI task.

The application should provide:
```
text
Stop Task
Cancel
Kill Running Command
Reject Plan
Approve Step
Approve All Safe Steps
```
---

## 17. Context Collection

The AI should receive enough context to be useful, but not unlimited or sensitive information.

Possible context sources:
```
text
Current working directory
Operating system information
Shell path
Selected terminal output
Recent command output
Selected files
Project tree
Package manager type
Git status
```
The user should control what context is shared with the AI.

Even though the model is local, the application should avoid including obvious secrets by default.

Avoid automatically sending:
```
text
SSH private keys
.env files
API tokens
password files
browser credentials
wallet files
full shell history
```
---

## 18. Prompting Strategy

The AI should be prompted to behave conservatively and produce structured responses when needed.

Example system behavior:
```
text
You are ZetaTerminal Agent, a local AI assistant running inside a Linux terminal application.

You help users diagnose and solve Linux, development, and system tasks.

Rules:
- Prefer read-only diagnostic commands first.
- Explain your plan before making changes.
- Do not hide commands from the user.
- Do not request destructive commands without clear justification.
- Ask for confirmation before sudo, deletion, package installation, service changes, permission changes, or network
download execution.
- Avoid interactive commands unless handing off to the user.
- Summarize what you did at the end.
```
For planning, structured JSON should be preferred:
```
json
{
  "summary": "Diagnose why Docker is not starting.",
  "risk": "medium",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "description": "Check Docker service status.",
      "command": "systemctl status docker --no-pager",
      "risk": "read_only",
      "requires_confirmation": false
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "description": "Inspect recent Docker logs.",
      "command": "journalctl -u docker --no-pager -n 100",
      "risk": "read_only",
      "requires_confirmation": false
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "description": "Restart Docker if diagnostics indicate it is appropriate.",
      "command": "sudo systemctl restart docker",
      "risk": "high",
      "requires_confirmation": true
    }
  ]
}
```
---

## 19. User Interface Concept

A practical layout:
```
text
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Menu / Toolbar                                                │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Terminal Tabs                                      AI Panel   │
│ ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐ ┌─────────┐ │
│ │                                              │ │ Ask AI  │ │
│ │ Real terminal session                        │ │         │ │
│ │                                              │ │ Plan    │ │
│ │                                              │ │ Actions │ │
│ └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └─────────┘ │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Status: Ollama connected · llama3.1:8b · Safe mode enabled    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```
The terminal should remain the primary interface.

The AI panel should provide:

- Plain-language input
- Context selection
- Plan display
- Risk labels
- Approval buttons
- Execution log
- Final summary

---

## 20. Persistent Settings

ZetaTerminal should remember user preferences.

Recommended settings:
```
text
app.windowGeometry
app.windowState
terminal.fontFamily
terminal.fontSize
terminal.shell
terminal.scrollbackLines
terminal.colorScheme
terminal.startDirectory
ai.enabled
ai.endpoint
ai.model
ai.autonomyMode
ai.allowReadOnlyAutoRun
ai.requireConfirmationForSudo
ai.requireConfirmationForDeletion
ai.maxContextBytes
ai.maxCommandOutputBytes
```
Settings should be validated on load. Invalid settings should fall back to safe defaults.

---

## 21. Logging and Diagnostics

ZetaTerminal should include logs for debugging and auditability.

Recommended log location:
```
text
~/.local/state/zeta-terminal/zeta-terminal.log
```
Useful log events:
```
text
Application start
Qt version
Terminal backend
Shell path
Ollama endpoint
Selected model
Settings loaded
Terminal session started
Terminal session ended
AI task started
AI plan generated
Command approved
Command executed
Command exit code
AI task completed
Errors and exceptions
```
Sensitive data should not be logged by default.

---

